  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Consider the function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>∞</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>→</mo><mi>R</mi></math> defined by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mi>x</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow></msup></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi></math> be respectively the number of points at which <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi></math> is not continuous and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi></math> is not differentiable, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></math> is</p>",
    "image": null
  },
